>
  <div class="quick-answer">
    <div class="quick-answer-label">Quick Answer</div>
    <p>In retirement, your estate plan shifts from protection to legacy. This stage is about <a href="/services/charitable-planning">charitable planning</a> (CRTs, CLTs, donor-advised funds), multigenerational wealth transfer (dynasty trusts, generation-skipping strategies), annual plan reviews to keep everything current, and clear communication with your family about your wishes. If you haven't reviewed your plan in the last three years, now is the time.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <p>Retirement is the first time in most people's lives when they can think about estate planning without the urgency of young children, growing businesses, or career demands. The plan you have — if you have one — was probably designed for a different stage. Now is the time to make it reflect who you are and what matters most to you.</p>

    <p>For some clients, legacy means ensuring their grandchildren are educated and their family is provided for across generations. For others, it means supporting charitable causes that align with their values. For most, it's some combination. The common thread is intentionality — making sure your assets do what you want them to do, not what default law dictates.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>Legacy Planning Strategies</h2>
    <p><strong>Charitable Remainder Trust (CRT).</strong> A CRT provides you with income during your lifetime and directs the remainder to a charity of your choice at death. It offers an upfront charitable deduction, removes the asset from your taxable estate, and can convert appreciated assets (like stock or real estate) into an income stream without triggering capital gains tax on the sale.</p>

    <p><strong>Charitable Lead Trust (CLT).</strong> The reverse of a CRT — the charity receives income first, and the remainder passes to your family. A CLT can be an effective way to transfer wealth to the next generation at reduced gift tax cost while supporting causes you care about during your lifetime.</p>

    <p><strong>Donor-Advised Fund (DAF).</strong> A simpler alternative to a private foundation. You make an irrevocable contribution to the fund, receive an immediate tax deduction, and direct grants to charities over time. A DAF can be established during your lifetime or funded at death through your trust.</p>

    <p><strong>Dynasty Trust.</strong> A trust designed to last for multiple generations — in Arizona, potentially forever, as the state has abolished the rule against perpetuities for trusts. A dynasty trust keeps assets out of each generation's taxable estate while providing for descendants. It's the most powerful tool for multigenerational wealth preservation.</p>

    <p><strong>Annual Gifting.</strong> The annual gift tax exclusion ($19,000 per recipient in 2026) allows you to transfer wealth to children and grandchildren without tax consequences. For married couples, that's $38,000 per recipient per year. Over a decade, strategic annual gifting can transfer substantial wealth outside your taxable estate.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>The Annual Review</h2>
    <p>We recommend annual estate plan reviews for all retired clients. Life changes — health, family dynamics, tax law, asset values — and a plan that was current last year may not be current today. An annual review catches these changes before they become problems.</p>

    <p>Topics we cover in an annual review include changes in health or capacity, changes in family circumstances (births, deaths, marriages, divorces), changes in assets or beneficiary designations, updates to Arizona or federal law, trustee and agent availability, and charitable giving intentions.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>Talking to Your Family</h2>
    <p>One of the most valuable things you can do at this stage is communicate your plan to the people affected by it. This doesn't mean sharing every detail — it means making sure your trustee knows they're named and understands the role, your healthcare agent knows your wishes, your children know where your documents are stored, and everyone understands the general structure well enough to avoid surprises.</p>

    <p>We can facilitate family meetings as part of the planning process. Having the conversation with an attorney present often makes it easier for everyone.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>What It Costs</h2>
    <p>Legacy planning engagements are flat-fee, quoted after your <a href="/contact">free consultation</a>. For clients who want ongoing annual reviews, we offer review subscriptions that keep your plan current year after year.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-section">
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          Is it too late to start charitable planning?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>No. Many charitable strategies — including CRTs, CLTs, donor-advised funds, and charitable bequests in your trust — can be implemented at any point. Some strategies, like a CRT funded with appreciated assets, are most effective when you have low-basis assets that would trigger significant capital gains if sold. We assess your situation and recommend the approach that provides the most benefit to both you and the causes you support.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          What happens to my trust when I die?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Your successor trustee takes over and administers the trust according to its terms — distributing assets to beneficiaries, paying debts and taxes, and managing any ongoing trusts (like trusts for minor grandchildren or a dynasty trust). If the trust is well-drafted and your trustee is prepared, this process happens privately, without probate, and according to your wishes.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          How do I make sure my grandchildren benefit from my estate plan?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Several ways: direct bequests in your trust, education trusts (including 529 plan contributions), dynasty trusts that span generations, and annual gifting during your lifetime. If you want to provide for grandchildren while keeping assets protected — from creditors, divorce, or their own inexperience — a trust structure is the right tool. We design the distribution terms around your values.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          What's the difference between a dynasty trust and a regular trust?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>A regular revocable trust terminates after distributing assets to your beneficiaries. A <a href="/glossary#dynasty-trust">dynasty trust</a> is designed to last for multiple generations — in Arizona, potentially indefinitely. Assets stay in the trust, protected from each generation's creditors, divorces, and estate taxes, while providing distributions for each generation's health, education, and support. It's the most powerful tool for preserving family wealth across generations.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <script>
    function toggleFaq(btn) {
      const item = btn.closest('.faq-item');
      const isOpen = item.classList.contains('open');
      document.querySelectorAll('.faq-item.open').forEach(el => el.classList.remove('open'));
      if (!isOpen) item.classList.add('open');
    }
  </script>
